    "title": "DIY Microbiome Sequencing: From Sample to Data",
    "excerpt": "How I sequenced my gut microbiome using Oxford Nanopore's MinION and analyzed the results with open-source bioinformatics tools.",
    "date": "2026-05-05",
    "category": "genomics",
    "tags": ["Microbiome", "Nanopore", "Sequencing", "Bioinformatics", "Gut Health"],
    "author": "GenomeTinker",
    "readTime": 18,
    "content": "# DIY Microbiome Sequencing: From Sample to Data\n\n## Overview\nThe Oxford Nanopore MinION has made DNA sequencing portable and affordable. Here's how I used it to sequence my own gut microbiome.\n\n## Sample Collection\n- Fecal sample collected using OMNIgene GUT kit\n- Stored at room temperature for up to 60 days\n- DNA extracted using QIAamp PowerFecal Pro kit\n\n## Library Preparation\n1. Quantify DNA with Qubit fluorometer\n2. Fragment DNA to ~8kb using g-TUBE\n3. End-prep and adapter ligation\n4. Load onto R9.4.1 flow cell\n\n## Sequencing Run\n- Platform: MinION Mk1B\n- Flow cell: R9.4.1\n- Run time: 48 hours\n- Total output: 2.3 Gb\n- N50: 6.8 kb\n\n## Bioinformatics Pipeline\n```bash\n# Basecalling with Guppy\nont-guppy --input fast5/ --output fastq/\n\n# Taxonomic classification with Kraken2\nkraken2 --db kraken2_db --output kraken.out fastq/reads.fastq\n\n# Visualization with Krona\nktImportTaxonomy kraken.out -o microbiome.html\n```\n\n## Results\nIdentified 347 species across 12 phyla. Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratio: 2.1 (within healthy range).",
